# TODO - Shared capability ownership

## Objective

Enforce reuse before new implementations.

## Acceptance

- Keep one canonical shared mechanism with configurable behaviour.
- Use specialised strategies only for real title-specific differences.
- Add regression tests across consumers when shared contracts change.

## Evidence

On 2026-11-24, `cargo validate --no-cache` passed after adding the
canonical `games::timing::StepClock` mechanism. Its interval and per-frame
work budget are configured once per scheduler; `FixedTick` composes different
consumers without forking the implementation. Synthetic counter and motion
consumers verify equivalent simulation under different frame rates, bounded
catch-up without lost time, ordered indices and invalid interval rejection.

The project has no onboarded titles yet. Title-specific specialisations are
therefore deferred until genuine differences can be demonstrated; no original
game parity is claimed. Cross-consumer regression tests protect the public
shared scheduler as its contract evolves.
